::JOUR_U06_L08_UA_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.ETH.01] Source: Final Journalism Capstone Scenario. Task: The student newsroom is submitting the final course portfolio and publication package. Required evidence: a revised article, a verified reporting note, a media-literacy audience check, a multimedia sequence, a collaborative publication workflow, a final online portfolio page, and a short oral presentation outline. Current Risks: one article transition is weak, one claim needs clearer evidence reasoning, one media headline overstates the audience opinion, one photo caption lacks date context, and the team has not assigned final export and submission roles. Goal: submit one accurate, revised, audience-ready, publication-quality journalism portfolio that demonstrates skills from the full course. Question: Unit Assessment Question 1: Which answer best applies Journalism course skills to the final capstone scenario? {
=A. Use evidence, revision, media literacy, accurate visuals, teamwork, publication workflow, and presentation organization to correct the final portfolio#Correct. Step 1: connect the answer to the MLA journalism standard. Step 2: use the embedded source to identify the reporting, writing, media, publication, collaboration, presentation, or evidence issue. Step 3: choose the option that protects accuracy, audience trust, organization, and publication readiness.
~B. Submit because most pieces are finished#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source instead of relying on a general impression. Step 2: identify the exact evidence, audience, revision, media, presentation, or workflow requirement. Step 3: reject choices that skip verification, ignore audience needs, weaken cohesion, or treat completion as mastery.
~C. Choose the most dramatic language even if it overstates evidence#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source instead of relying on a general impression. Step 2: identify the exact evidence, audience, revision, media, presentation, or workflow requirement. Step 3: reject choices that skip verification, ignore audience needs, weaken cohesion, or treat completion as mastery.
~D. Ignore team roles because individual work matters more#Not correct. Step 1: reread the embedded source instead of relying on a general impression. Step 2: identify the exact evidence, audience, revision, media, presentation, or workflow requirement. Step 3: reject choices that skip verification, ignore audience needs, weaken cohesion, or treat completion as mastery.
}
